<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garsenda of Forcalquier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1180–c. 1242</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Garsenda was countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a>, the granddaughter and heiress of Count William IV. Her marriage in 1193 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-count-of-provence">Alfonso II, Count of Provence</a>, brought Forcalquier back into union with Provence, and their son Ramon Berenguer IV ruled both. She is also counted among the trobairitz, the women troubadours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a>, and was a patron of poets.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garsenda-of-forcalquier.jpg" alt="Garsenda of Forcalquier" width="280" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Blancard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gersenda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Garsenda was the daughter of Rainou, or Rénier, lord of Caylar and Ansouis of the House of Sabran, and of Garsenda, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-forcalquier">William IV of Forcalquier</a>. Her mother was William's heiress but died before him, so the inheritance passed to the daughter. Garsenda was only thirteen when her grandfather and Alfonso II signed the Treaty of Aix, under which she would inherit his county and marry Alfonso. The wedding took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a> in July 1193. The couple had at least two children: a son and heir, and a daughter, also Garsenda, who married Guillermo II de Montcada and was the mother of Gaston VII, Viscount of Béarn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>William IV and Alfonso both died in 1209, leaving Garsenda the natural guardian of her son. Her brother-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, first gave the regency of Provence to his brother Sancho, who passed Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> to his own son Nuño Sánchez after Peter's death in 1213. Garsenda's partisans accused Nuño of trying to supplant his nephew. The Provençal nobles at length lined up behind her and removed him, and the regency passed to Garsenda with a council of native nobles. By 1217 or 1220 she had ceded Forcalquier to her son and handed over the government. She retired to the monastery of La Celle around 1225.</p><p>Probably during her regency she was the focus of a circle of poets. A tenso between a good lady, named in one chansonnier as the countess of Provence, and an anonymous troubadour opens with her lines Vos q'em semblatz dels corals amadors, in which she declares her love. Some read the troubadour as Gui de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavaillon">Cavaillon</a>, whose vida repeats a probably unfounded rumour that he was her lover. Elias de Barjols wrote songs about her as a widow, and Raimon Vidal praised her patronage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1242 Garsenda travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> to see her newly born great-granddaughter, Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the child's parents, bringing sixty knights to the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, who was then at war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Her most recent editors have called her one of the most powerful women in Occitan history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Garsenda, Countess of Forcalquier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1193 William IV's granddaughter <b>Garsenda</b> married Alfonso II of Provence, and on William's death in 1209 Forcalquier was reunited with the County of Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forcalquier">County of Forcalquier</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Provence, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in La Celle, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44,6&amp;year=1220" aria-label="Provence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Garsenda of Forcalquier"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5368 3805h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5368 3805h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44,6&amp;year=1220">What was here in 1220? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
